\section{End-Effector-Only SDF Cannot Ensure Full-Body Safety}\label{sec:ee_only}

Could a perfect SDF signal enable effective EE-only refinement? We replace the learned SDF with ground-truth distance fields from the simulation geometry (\S\ref{sec:oracle_setup}). Table~\ref{tab:oracle} and Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_bar} show that all three oracle variants achieve \emph{lower} CAR than unrefined baselines: the best oracle (76.9\%) falls 5.1 points below LoRA (82.0\%). Refinement with perfect distance information produces more collisions, not fewer.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Oracle SDF refinement with ground-truth distance fields. All oracle variants achieve lower CAR than unrefined baselines. Per-task breakdown in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_pertask_oracle}.}
\label{tab:oracle}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & CAR (\%) & Collapsed (\%) & Refines/ep \\
\midrule
LoRA baseline (no refine) & 82.0 & 99.4 & -- \\
Full-FT baseline (no refine) & 83.0 & -- & -- \\
\midrule
Oracle-adaptive (GT, dyn.\ $\alpha$) & 75.0 & 98.3 & 14.2 \\
Oracle-fixed (GT, $\alpha{=}0.005$) & 73.1 & 98.3 & 14.8 \\
Oracle-feature (GT, feat.\ space) & 76.9 & 96.7 & 241.7 \\
\midrule
A3 learned SDF & 77.1 & -- & 0.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{fig_oracle_bar.pdf}
\caption{CAR for oracle SDF refinement versus unrefined baselines. All GT oracle variants underperform baselines despite perfect distance information.}
\label{fig:oracle_bar}
\end{figure}

Per-task analysis reveals the mechanism (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_pertask_oracle}). On the one task where the EE is the primary collision body, oracle refinement helps (+9 points). On the remaining three, the SDF gradient pushes the EE away from obstacles but rotates intermediate arm links into obstacle volumes; the net effect is one collision traded for another. The A3 learned SDF never triggers refinement (0 steps per episode). EE-only refinement is structurally counterproductive: joint-space coupling between EE displacement and arm-link rotation redistributes collisions to unmonitored links regardless of signal quality. The ecological validity of this finding varies across configurations: the Pi0+A1 baseline (TSR$\,\approx\,$0\%) is already fully action-collapsed, making its oracle test a degenerate case. The LoRA baseline is itself largely action-collapsed (99.4\% collapsed episodes, Table~\ref{tab:oracle}); the structural finding---that EE-only refinement redistributes rather than reduces collisions---should hold regardless of base-policy activity level, but validation on a functional (non-collapsed) policy remains future work.

